    {
      "id": "KA-C02-enu",
      "sequence": null,
      "title": "ಏನು (ēnu) — \"what\"",
      "headword": "ಏನು",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "what",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b9d4879e03664154",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last piece of the question: \"what.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(Skim if you read Kannada.) ಏ (independent long ē, word-initial) + ನು (nu) becomes ēnu."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಏನು (ēnu, \"what\") is native Dravidian, on the interrogative stem yā- / e- — the same Dravidian question-root as Tamil eṉṉa, heading Kannada's question family: ēnu (what), yāru (who), elli (where), yāke (why)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"ēnu\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"ēnu\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the question family — ēnu, yāru, elli",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the question family — ēnu, yāru, elli]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Dravidian stem heads ಏನು and Kannada's other question-words? (yā-/e-, the same as Tamil eṉṉa.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "KA-C02-hesaru",
      "sequence": null,
      "title": "ಹೆಸರು (hesaru) — \"name,\" and the p becomes h shift",
      "headword": "ಹೆಸರು",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "name",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2e55e2b9599e2e3d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"Name\" — and a word that shows off a famous Kannada sound-law."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(Skim if you read Kannada.) ಹ (ha) + ೆ (the e-sign) becomes ಹೆ (he); ಸ (sa); ರು (ru). Read ಹೆ, ಸ, ರು becomes hesaru."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಹೆಸರು (hesaru, \"name\") is native Dravidian, from Proto-Dravidian pesar — and it hides a famous Kannada sound-law: an old initial p became h. So pesar becomes hesar becomes hesaru. That makes it a cousin of Tamil peyar (same root; Tamil kept the p, Kannada softened it to h) — and, like peyar, not related to Sanskrit nāma (Hindi nām, English name)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Across the family: Kannada hesaru, Tamil peyar, Telugu pēru, Malayalam pēr — one Dravidian word; only Hindi nām / English name are Indo-European."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"hesaru\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"hesaru\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the shift — pesar becomes hesaru (p becomes h)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the shift — *\\*pesar* → *hesaru* (p → h)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "its Tamil cousin — peyar",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: its Tamil cousin — *peyar*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What sound-law turned pesar into hesaru, and what Tamil word is its cousin? (p becomes h; Tamil peyar.) Is it related to English name? (No — native Dravidian.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "KA-C02-nanna",
      "sequence": null,
      "title": "ನನ್ನ (nanna) — \"my\"",
      "headword": "ನನ್ನ",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "my",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:25ce7bc99a448aa3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"My\" — native Dravidian, with no European cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(Skim if you read Kannada.) ನ (na) + the conjunct ನ್ನ (nna) becomes nanna — a doubled, held nn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ನನ್ನ (nanna, \"my\") is the possessive of ನಾನು (nānu, \"I\") — native Dravidian, on the pronoun stem nān, the same one behind Tamil nāṉ / eṉ. It has no Indo-European cousin: the Dravidian \"I/my\" is a different word from the European I/my."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"nanna\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"nanna\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "its source — nānu (\"I\") becomes nanna (\"my\")",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: its source — *nānu* (\"I\") → *nanna* (\"my\")]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What word is ನನ್ನ the possessive of? (nānu, \"I\".) Which Tamil pronoun shares its root? (nāṉ.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "KA-C02-nanna-hesaru",
      "sequence": null,
      "title": "ನನ್ನ ಹೆಸರು … (nanna hesaru …) — \"my name is…,\" with no \"is\"",
      "headword": "ನನ್ನ ಹೆಸರು …",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "my name is… (with no \"is\")",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e51b27c4418575da",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Assemble the two atoms — and notice, as in Tamil, no word for \"is.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The phrase, assembled",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nanna (my) + hesaru (name) + your name."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ನನ್ನ ಹೆಸರು … = \"my name …\" becomes \"my name is…\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nanna hesaru Arun. becomes \"My name is Arun.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the zero copula",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There is no verb. Kannada sets \"my name\" beside \"Arun\" and lets the \"is\" be understood — the zero copula of the Dravidian family (just like Tamil). Nanna hesaru Arun = literally \"my name Arun.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"nanna hesaru …\" with your name",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"nanna hesaru …\" with your name]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "notice — no word for \"is\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: notice — no word for \"is\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Give your name in Kannada. (Nanna hesaru ….) What does Kannada leave out that English needs? (The verb \"is\" — zero copula.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "KA-C02-niinu-niivu",
      "sequence": null,
      "title": "ನೀನು / ನೀವು (nīnu / nīvu) — \"you,\" familiar and respectful",
      "headword": "ನೀನು / ನೀವು",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "you (familiar / respectful)",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8d2a0f5bd8c72e49",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"You\" — and Kannada makes respect the same way Tamil and French do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(Skim if you read Kannada.) ನೀ (nī) + ನು (nu) becomes nīnu; ನೀ + ವು (vu) becomes nīvu."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ನೀನು (nīnu, familiar \"you\") from Proto-Dravidian nīn — the same native root as Tamil nī. ನೀವು (nīvu) is the respectful/plural \"you.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: respect by the plural",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nīnu (familiar) vs. nīvu (respectful, and literally plural) — the same politeness-by-plural as Tamil nīṅgaḷ and French vous: one respected person addressed as \"you all.\" For a first meeting, nīvu."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"nīnu\" (familiar), \"nīvu\" (respectful)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"nīnu\" (familiar), \"nīvu\" (respectful)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "How does Kannada make \"you\" respectful, and which languages use the same trick? (By the plural — nīvu; Tamil nīṅgaḷ, French vous.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
